steps.detail:
An owner who has not paid after 14 days is a 'defaulter'. The body may then:
- display a defaulters' list on the notice boards, updated monthly;
- deactivate the defaulter's access card or tag without prior notice, with a reactivation charge of not more than RM50;
- suspend the defaulter's use of common facilities, including a designated car park bay;
- agree a written instalment scheme;
- accept payment from the defaulter's tenant, occupier or chargee.
If the owner is in arrears on the 7th day before a meeting, the owner cannot vote and cannot stand for election to the committee.

If the sum is not paid within 14 days of attachment, enough of the property to realise the sum is sold by auction under the Commissioner's supervision. A licensed auctioneer may be appointed. The seller must:
- fix a reserve price and accept only bids at or above it;
- give at least 7 days' public notice stating the date, the property and the reserve price (the Commissioner may approve a shorter period in writing);
- hold the auction in a place open to the public.
The highest bidder is declared the purchaser, gets a receipt and becomes the absolute owner. Private treaty sale needs the proprietor's consent. The Commissioner need not attend.

Proceeds go first to the sum due plus the costs of attachment and sale. These costs include the RM20 filing fee, any livestock maintenance, auctioneer, advertising, watchman and custody costs, and an administrative charge of RM300 or 3% of the amount due, whichever is higher. Within 7 days after the sale, Form 22 is served by registered post. The defaulter must:
- collect any surplus within 30 days, or it becomes an advance payment towards charges and the sinking fund;
- collect any unsold items within 7 days, or pay storage charges;
- pay any shortfall immediately.
